d on this session's environment, and the network proxy injects it into requests to that host from every session in that environment, including other people's sessions when the environment is shared: this session usually within a few seconds, and later sessions. Saving it also allows HTTPS requests to that host from those sessions, so do not add the host to the environment's allowed hosts for it. If get_environment is among your tools, first make the move its description calls for, unless the user asked for the credential here. If the credential will be saved on the user's Default all the same, because the session has no repository or could not be moved, say so in your message, then call this tool, and do not propose a move for a credential again in this session. If the user declined the move, call this tool only if they then ask for the credential. You never receive the value — the result only says whether a credential is now configured, and if not, why. If the call itself is denied, because the user declined or because the app they are using cannot show the form, no result comes back: do not call this again for that host, and tell them the credential can be added in the environment's settings, then retry the failing request once it has been. After `saved`, retry the failing request right away. Until the proxy picks the credential up, the request can still fail with 401/403 or with `request blocked: no rule allows host`: if it is refused again with one of those, wait about a minute and retry once more rather than change network settings. Do not wait for the proxy after a timeout or any other error. This cannot help with secrets that must exist inside the sandbox (database passwords, container registry pulls, SSH keys), GitHub (use the repository tools), or public package registries the proxy does not handle. A local key check is no reason to skip this tool. If a CLI or SDK will not start without a key and sends it in a header, give it a dummy value in the format it 